yang/php/antlr/quoted.cljc\\\\nsrc/cljc/yang/php/antlr/safepoint.cljc\\\\nsrc/cljc/yang/php/antlr/scope.cljc\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_9\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"nl -ba docs/design/yang.php.md | sed -n '105,210p;350,375p;1400,1435p;1590,1630p;1920,1970p;2090,2135p;2425,2460p'\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"   105\\\\tpinning `precision=14`, `serialize_precision=-1`, `error_reporting=E_ALL` and\\\\n   106\\\\t`display_errors=stderr`. The grammar's feature set (enums, `match`,\\\\n   107\\\\t`readonly`, named arguments, attributes) is PHP 8.1 and later, so 8.3 is the\\\\n   108\\\\tnatural pin. PHP 8.4 features (property hooks, asymmetric visibility) are not\\\\n   109\\\\tparsed (corner C10).\\\\n   110\\\\t\\\\n   111\\\\t### 3.2 Arrays and keys\\\\n   112\\\\t\\\\n   113\\\\tA PHP array is a value: assignment and parameter passing copy it, and a write\\\\n   114\\\\tnever affects another holder. Its representation is chosen by what it contains\\\\n   115\\\\t(section 4.2): an array that transitively holds no reference, no\\\\n   116\\\\tarray-instance handle and no object handle is a **plain** persistent ordered\\\\n   117\\\\tmap stored directly in its holder, copied in O(1), with mutation as get, pure\\\\n   118\\\\tupdate and `cell/set!`. Any other array is a counted **instance** cell whose\\\\n   119\\\\tduplication follows the engine (section 4).\\\\n   120\\\\t\\\\n   121\\\\t- **Key coercion** at every write and read: an integer stays; a\\\\n   122\\\\t  decimal-integer string in canonical form (`\\\\\\\"8\\\\\\\"` yes; `\\\\\\\"08\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\"8.0\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\" 8\\\\\\\"`\\\\n   123\\\\t  no) becomes that integer; a float truncates toward zero (with the PHP 8.1\\\\n   124\\\\t  deprecation emitted as a diagnostic record); `true` and `false` become 1 and\\\\n   125\\\\t  0; `null` becomes `\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"`; any other string stays a string.\\\\n   126\\\\t- `next-index` is one more than the largest integer key ever used and never\\\\n   127\\\\t  decreases on unset; negative keys follow PHP 8.3's rule (next is max + 1).\\\\n   128\\\\t  `$a[] = v` appends at `next-index`. The one decrement is `array_pop`, which\\\\n   129\\\\t  lowers it when the popped key was `next-index - 1`.\\\\n   130\\\\t- Iteration follows the key order (`php/array-keys`; the prelude's array\\\\n   131\\\\t  operations are the only code that reads `:index`, `:order` or `:next-index`).\\\\n   132\\\\t  Unset splices the order. Nested arrays are nested\\\\n   133\\\\t  values: `$a['x']['y'] = 1` is one path update.\\\\n   134\\\\t- Large arrays cost one path copy per write; a packed-vector fast path is an\\\\n   135\\\\t  attached optimizer's business, not the lowering's.\\\\n   136\\\\t\\\\n   137\\\\t#### 3.2.1 Reads, `isset`, `empty`, `unset` and builtins (P2)\\\\n   138\\\\t\\\\n   139\\\\t- **Reads.** A missing key warns (2) `Undefined array key \\\\\\\"x\\\\\\\"` or\\\\n   140\\\\t  `Undefined array key 5` and answers null; an offset read on null, bool, int\\\\n   141\\\\t  or float warns `Trying to access array offset on <type|true|false>`; a\\\\n   142\\\\t  string container answers the one byte at the offset (negative counts from the\\\\n   143\\\\t  end), out of range warns `Uninitialized string offset N` and answers `\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"`.\\\\n   144\\\\t  `$a[]` in read position is a compile error `Cannot use [] for reading`.\\\\n   145\\\\t- **`isset`/`empty` on a string container** coerce the offset, then test the\\\\n   146\\\\t  range: int itself; `true`, `false`, null as `1`, `0`, `0` silently; a float\\\\n   147\\\\t  by the float-key seam (`php/float-key`, precision deprecation when\\\\n   148\\\\t  fractional, no \\\\\\\"String offset cast\\\\\\\" warning, which belongs to reads); an\\\\n   149\\\\t  integer-numeric string as that int; anything else is not an offset (`isset`\\\\n   150\\\\t  false, `empty` true).\\\\n   151\\\\t- **`unset` on a non-array**: a missing key or null at any level is a no-op;\\\\n   152\\\\t  a string terminal is `Error \\\\\\\"Cannot unset string offsets\\\\\\\"`; int, float and\\\\n   153\\\\t  `true` are `Error \\\\\\\"Cannot unset offset in a non-array variable\\\\\\\"`; `false`\\\\n   154\\\\t  emits Deprecated (8192) `Automatic conversion of false to array is\\\\n   155\\\\t  deprecated` and stays `false`.\\\\n   156\\\\t- **Builtin protocol.** One table (`yang.php.antlr.builtins/supported`) feeds\\\\n   157\\\\t  the prelude registration and the lowering's by-reference positions. For a\\\\n   158\\\\t  call with `:by-ref` positions the lowering (1) binds each by-ref location's\\\\n   159\\\\t  keys at that argument's place in left-to-right order; (2) evaluates every\\\\n   160\\\\t  other argument left to right into temporaries (a non-location at a by-ref\\\\n   161\\\\t  position warns 8 `Only variables should be passed by reference`); (3)\\\\n   162\\\\t  immediately before the call, reads each by-ref location through its bound\\\\n   163\\\\t  keys and builds the argument vector; (4) calls; (5) writes each out back\\\\n   164\\\\t  through the same keys, in position order.\\\\n   165\\\\t- **`foreach` by value** iterates a snapshot (`php/foreach-array`, then\\\\n   166\\\\t  `php/array-keys` and `php/array-get`), so writes, `unset`, appends and\\\\n   167\\\\t  `sort` of the source in the body never change the iteration (the by-value\\\\n   168\\\\t  half of C15). A non-array warns and iterates nothing.\\\\n   169\\\\t- **Sorting** is one stable merge sort in the prelude (no host sort), with\\\\n   170\\\\t  flags `SORT_REGULAR`, `SORT_NUMERIC`, `SORT_STRING`; `r*` variants flip the\\\\n   171\\\\t  sign without reversing equal elements. A **bool** comparator is retried\\\\n   172\\\\t  swapped and emits Deprecated 8192 `usort(): Returning bool from comparison\\\\n   173\\\\t  function is deprecated, return an integer less than, equal to, or greater\\\\n   174\\\\t  than zero` once per call. Parity claim: `usort` with a bool comparator\\\\n   175\\\\t  `return $a > $b;` over `[3, 1, 2]` matches the CLI's order and emits the\\\\n   176\\\\t  deprecation exactly once per call. Stability is not parity for a comparator\\\\n   177\\\\t  that disagrees with itself (corner G-sort-inconsistent-cmp, section 17.2).\\\\n   178\\\\t\\\\n   179\\\\t### 3.3 Scope\\\\n   180\\\\t\\\\n   181\\\\t- PHP has function scope only, with no block scope and no implicit closure\\\\n   182\\\\t  capture, except arrow `fn`, which captures by value every enclosing variable\\\\n   183\\\\t  it mentions, at creation.\\\\n   184\\\\t- **Locals are cells** allocated at function entry with `:php/undef`; reading\\\\n   185\\\\t  one emits `Warning: Undefined variable` and yields null. Every function body\\\\n   186\\\\t  that declares locals or parameters releases them on exit (row 16).\\\\n   187\\\\t- `global $x` binds the local to the `$GLOBALS` entry by reference (row 17),\\\\n   188\\\\t  creating it as null if absent.\\\\n   189\\\\t- `static $x = <expr>;` is a cell owned by the function object, allocated at\\\\n   190\\\\t  function creation with an uninitialized sentinel. Its initializer is an\\\\n   191\\\\t  arbitrary expression evaluated **when execution first reaches the\\\\n   192\\\\t  declaration** (PHP 8.3) and filled once.\\\\n   193\\\\t- **The dynamic-scope profile**: a function whose body contains\\\\n   194\\\\t  `include`/`require`, `$$name`, `extract`, `compact`, `get_defined_vars`,\\\\n   195\\\\t  `eval`, or one-argument `parse_str` keeps its locals in a heap symbol-table\\\\n   196\\\\t  dict (the precedent is Python's module namespace, owner decision 1 of\\\\n   197\\\\t  `yang.antlr.md` 8.5.1). Scope analysis decides per function, and the\\\\n   198\\\\t  lowering targets `php/local-get`/`php/local-set` on the dict. All other\\\\n   199\\\\t  functions use cells (owner decision B8.3). The symbol table holds reference\\\\n   200\\\\t  markers like any location.\\\\n   201\\\\t- **The global scope** is always the `$GLOBALS` dict: a heap dict,\\\\n   202\\\\t  insertion-ordered, with the superglobals pre-seeded from the request record.\\\\n   203\\\\t  `$GLOBALS` is read-only as of PHP 8.1 except through its elements (corner\\\\n   204\\\\t  C13).\\\\n   205\\\\t\\\\n   206\\\\t### 3.4 Superglobals and the request context\\\\n   207\\\\t\\\\n   208\\\\t- A PHP run is one task consuming one **request record** from a\\\\n   209\\\\t  composition-supplied stream: `{:php.request/method :uri :query :headers\\\\n   210\\\\t  :cookies :body :server :env :files}`. `php/init!` builds `$_GET`, `$_POST`,\\\\n   350\\\\t### 3.11 Mixed HTML mode\\\\n   351\\\\t\\\\n   352\\\\t`inlineHtml` tokens lower to `php/echo` of the literal text in source order\\\\n   353\\\\tbetween code blocks. `?>` swallows one following newline (the lexer's rule,\\\\n   354\\\\tpreserved through the span); the first `<?php` has no output before it unless\\\\n   355\\\\tthere is text. Output order is statement order: exit criterion 1 is a direct\\\\n   356\\\\tfixture.\\\\n   357\\\\t\\\\n   358\\\\t### 3.12 eval\\\\n   359\\\\t\\\\n   360\\\\t`eval` is a new compilation request (`yang.antlr.md` 8.6) sharing the caller's\\\\n   361\\\\tscope, so it forces the dynamic-scope profile on its function. It is refused\\\\n   362\\\\twith `:yang.php/unsupported :eval` until its slice (P9).\\\\n   363\\\\t\\\\n   364\\\\t## 4. The reference, array and object lifecycle\\\\n   365\\\\t\\\\n   366\\\\tPHP's observable aliasing (references, copy-on-write separation that unwraps\\\\n   367\\\\tunshared references, destructor timing) is defined by the Zend engine's\\\\n   368\\\\toperations, so this model is derived from them, not from an abstraction. Each\\\\n   369\\\\trow names the Zend handler it maps to; each row is normative and is confirmed\\\\n   370\\\\tagainst the pinned PHP CLI before the slice that claims it lands (the blocking\\\\n   371\\\\trule, section 17.3).\\\\n   372\\\\t\\\\n   373\\\\t### 4.1 What the engine does\\\\n   374\\\\t\\\\n   375\\\\t- A **zval** is a typed slot in a CV, a hashtable bucket, a property table or\\\\n  1400\\\\tlambdas with cells, which also makes safepoints and tail marks work.\\\\n  1401\\\\t\\\\n  1402\\\\tMigration mechanics (the F2 pattern):\\\\n  1403\\\\t\\\\n  1404\\\\t- The ANTLR frontend is installed in `yin.repl.frontends/standard` as a\\\\n  1405\\\\t  new revision of `:yang/php` (the compiler frontend keeps its revision\\\\n  1406\\\\t  during the migration window; `[id revision]` pins either), with no\\\\n  1407\\\\t  fallback.\\\\n  1408\\\\t- Every program in `test/yang/php_test.clj` is re-expressed in the new corpus,\\\\n  1409\\\\t  with expectations corrected where the legacy was wrong (`null` is\\\\n  1410\\\\t  `:php/null`, not `nil`).\\\\n  1411\\\\t- The browser demo `src/cljs/datomworld/demo/compilation_pipeline.cljs`\\\\n  1412\\\\t  compiles editable PHP synchronously in CLJS with the legacy frontend, while\\\\n  1413\\\\t  the local ANTLR parser is JVM-only until a Dart helper exists (amended\\\\n  1414\\\\t  2026-10-17: PHP has Java and JavaScript helpers upstream; per-target helper\\\\n  1415\\\\t  availability decides the host matrix, `yang.antlr.md` 4.2). The demo moves to **checked-in CST packets**\\\\n  1416\\\\t  for its sample programs (generated by a JVM script, the `dht-import-v1.edn`\\\\n  1417\\\\t  pattern), lowered and run parserless in the browser; editable PHP source in\\\\n  1418\\\\t  the demo is dropped, or routed to a configured parser service when one\\\\n  1419\\\\t  exists (owner decision B8.9). `src/dev/subsystems.edn` is a one-line\\\\n  1420\\\\t  retarget.\\\\n  1421\\\\t- The compiler revision of `:yang/php` retires at P8 (implementation\\\\n  1422\\\\t  deleted, no shim; the repository has no backward-compatibility\\\\n  1423\\\\t  obligation) once the demo retarget has landed and the corpus covers the\\\\n  1424\\\\t  compiler's programs (owner decision B8.6).\\\\n  1425\\\\t\\\\n  1426\\\\tWhat stays refused after migration: `goto`; return by reference (corner C7);\\\\n  1427\\\\t`declare(encoding)`; `__halt_compiler`; `eval` until its slice; `$$` and\\\\n  1428\\\\t`extract` until the dynamic-scope slice; extension surfaces (`ext/*`: PDO,\\\\n  1429\\\\tcurl, mbstring, gd, intl, openssl, sockets, `pcntl` beyond signals), each a\\\\n  1430\\\\tbound surface per ADR 0004; `preg_*` until a portable regex engine exists (a\\\\n  1431\\\\tPCRE-compatible engine is large and host engines diverge); `date`, `time`,\\\\n  1432\\\\t`microtime`, `rand`, `random_int` and `uniqid` exist only as effects with\\\\n  1433\\\\texplicit outcomes.\\\\n  1434\\\\t\\\\n  1435\\\\t## 6. The grammar route and parser worker\\\\n  1590\\\\t|            | methods; constructors with     |                                |\\\\n  1591\\\\t|            | promotion;                     |                                |\\\\n  1592\\\\t|            | `static`/`self`/`parent`;      |                                |\\\\n  1593\\\\t|            | interfaces; traits (`use`,     |                                |\\\\n  1594\\\\t|            | `insteadof`, `as`); enums      |                                |\\\\n  1595\\\\t|            | (pure and backed); attributes  |                                |\\\\n  1596\\\\t|            | (data only); magic methods;    |                                |\\\\n  1597\\\\t|            | `__toString` in string         |                                |\\\\n  1598\\\\t|            | contexts                       |                                |\\\\n  1599\\\\t+------------+--------------------------------+--------------------------------+\\\\n  1600\\\\t| Exceptions | `try`/`catch`/`finally`,       | handler stack with             |\\\\n  1601\\\\t|            | multi-catch, `throw`           | `php/raise`; `finally` via     |\\\\n  1602\\\\t|            | expressions, rethrow           | `php/try-finally`              |\\\\n  1603\\\\t+------------+--------------------------------+--------------------------------+\\\\n  1604\\\\t| Generators | `yield`, `yield k => v`,       | the C2 core under `php/gen-*`  |\\\\n  1605\\\\t|            | `yield from`; `send`, `throw`, |                                |\\\\n  1606\\\\t|            | `getReturn`                    |                                |\\\\n  1607\\\\t+------------+--------------------------------+--------------------------------+\\\\n  1608\\\\t| Modules    | `include`, `require`, `_once`; | section 11; ticks per section  |\\\\n  1609\\\\t|            | `namespace`, `use`;            | 12                             |\\\\n  1610\\\\t|            | `declare(strict_types)`,       |                                |\\\\n  1611\\\\t|            | `declare(ticks)`               |                                |\\\\n  1612\\\\t+------------+--------------------------------+--------------------------------+\\\\n  1613\\\\t| Refused    | `goto`; return by reference;   | `:yang.php/unsupported         |\\\\n  1614\\\\t|            | `eval` pending its run-time    | {:construct}`                  |\\\\n  1615\\\\t|            | compiler bridge; `$$`          |                                |\\\\n  1616\\\\t|            | references;                    |                                |\\\\n  1617\\\\t|            | `__halt_compiler`;             |                                |\\\\n  1618\\\\t|            | `declare(encoding)`; nested    |                                |\\\\n  1619\\\\t|            | destructuring by reference     |                                |\\\\n  1620\\\\t|            | beyond one level (corner C12)  |                                |\\\\n  1621\\\\t+------------+--------------------------------+--------------------------------+\\\\n  1622\\\\t\\\\n  1623\\\\t## 9. The prelude inventory\\\\n  1624\\\\t\\\\n  1625\\\\tModule `php` (reserved), hook prelude `phpsp`. `php/init!` builds the class\\\\n  1626\\\\ttable with the builtin classes and interfaces (`stdClass`, `Closure`,\\\\n  1627\\\\t`Generator`, `Traversable`, `Iterator`, `IteratorAggregate`, `ArrayAccess`,\\\\n  1628\\\\t`Countable`, `Stringable`, `JsonSerializable`, `UnitEnum`, `BackedEnum`, the\\\\n  1629\\\\t`Throwable` hierarchy; `ArrayObject` deferred), the function table with the\\\\n  1630\\\\tsupported builtins and their by-reference positions, `$GLOBALS` from the\\\\n  1920\\\\t   `:yang.php/refusal`.\\\\n  1921\\\\t\\\\n  1922\\\\t## 12. Safepoints\\\\n  1923\\\\t\\\\n  1924\\\\t- `:loop` and `:call` hooks (`phpsp`) serve signals: `pcntl_signal` handlers\\\\n  1925\\\\t  run at hooks via `stream/poll!` on a signal stream, and\\\\n  1926\\\\t  `pcntl_async_signals` is the default and only mode. A recursion-depth bound\\\\n  1927\\\\t  is available if a composition wants one (PHP has none; default off).\\\\n  1928\\\\t- `max_execution_time` and `set_time_limit` are wall-clock in PHP and so\\\\n  1929\\\\t  nondeterministic: the profile offers a deterministic **safepoint-count\\\\n  1930\\\\t  budget** instead, raising the fatal with PHP's text, a recorded departure\\\\n  1931\\\\t  (owner decision B8.5, corner C21). A composition may supply one non-negative\\\\n  1932\\\\t  integer budget per run; omission means unlimited. Each entry to a `:loop` or\\\\n  1933\\\\t  `:call` hook consumes one unit, and the hook after the last available unit\\\\n  1934\\\\t  raises `PHP Fatal error:  Maximum execution time of 30 seconds exceeded`.\\\\n  1935\\\\t  This operational fatal is not guest-catchable and skips shutdown functions\\\\n  1936\\\\t  and destructors. It bounds execution between hooks, not wall-clock time or\\\\n  1937\\\\t  blocking host operations; no generic VM instructions are charged.\\\\n  1938\\\\t- **Statement ticks** (`declare(ticks=N)`, `register_tick_function`) need\\\\n  1939\\\\t  `:line` sites before non-lambda statement nodes. `yang.safepoint` currently\\\\n  1940\\\\t  refuses a selected site that is not a `:lambda` (`site-not-lambda`), and\\\\n  1941\\\\t  `:line` insertion is safepoint slice 3, which Python tracing also needs.\\\\n  1942\\\\t  Ticks therefore **depend on the shared `:line` extension**: P7 splits into\\\\n  1943\\\\t  P7-a (signals, no dependency) and P7-b (ticks, behind safepoint slice 3).\\\\n  1944\\\\t  Neither L1 to L4 nor this frontend extends `yang.safepoint`; the extension\\\\n  1945\\\\t  is the safepoint seat's.\\\\n  1946\\\\t- No thread switches: PHP is single-threaded, and one request is one task.\\\\n  1947\\\\t\\\\n  1948\\\\t## 13. Diagnostics\\\\n  1949\\\\t\\\\n  1950\\\\tCompile time: `:yang.php/syntax`; `:yang.php/unsupported {:construct}`;\\\\n  1951\\\\t`:yang.php/not-php`; `:yang.php/unknown-builtin` (a warning);\\\\n  1952\\\\t`:yang.php/compile-error` for PHP's compile-time fatals (a function redeclared\\\\n  1953\\\\tin one unit, `break 0`, a duplicate parameter, `$this` reassignment, a\\\\n  1954\\\\tpositional argument after a named one). Run time: guest warnings, notices and\\\\n  1955\\\\tdeprecations as records on the guest-diagnostics stream; fatals as `Error`\\\\n  1956\\\\tthrows; the uncaught-exception text pinned. Operational failures stay\\\\n  1957\\\\toperational (`yang.antlr.md` section 11).\\\\n  1958\\\\t\\\\n  1959\\\\t## 14. The differential-corpus plan\\\\n  1960\\\\t\\\\n  1961\\\\t- **Oracle**: the pinned PHP 8.3.x CLI with the pinned ini. The comparison\\\\n  1962\\\\t  covers stdout and the diagnostics stream, rendered in PHP's `Warning: ... in\\\\n  1963\\\\t  <unit> on line N` form with the path replaced by the unit name and the line\\\\n  1964\\\\t  derived from the side table (owner decision 2 of `yang.antlr.md` 8.5.1\\\\n  1965\\\\t  reused), against PHP's stderr with paths normalized.\\\\n  1966\\\\t- **Groups**: juggling (every pair of the comparison matrix, numeric strings,\\\\n  1967\\\\t  arithmetic warnings and `TypeError`); arrays (key coercion, order, next\\\\n  1968\\\\t  index after unset, copy-on-write observable through references, nested\\\\n  1969\\\\t  writes, sort stability); references and lifecycle (section 4.6's three\\\\n  1970\\\\t  sources); strings (bytes, interpolation forms, heredoc indentation,\\\\n  2090\\\\t|       | `use`, constants, the autoload   | imports on Node and Dart with     |\\\\n  2091\\\\t|       | class map, publication and DHT   | equal output; the computed-path   |\\\\n  2092\\\\t|       | import parserless on Node and    | diagnostic; an unpublished target |\\\\n  2093\\\\t|       | Dart, the dynamic-scope profile  | refused at publication by name; a |\\\\n  2094\\\\t|       | for including functions          | pinned but unserved target:       |\\\\n  2095\\\\t|       |                                  | `include` warns and yields        |\\\\n  2096\\\\t|       |                                  | `false`, `require` and            |\\\\n  2097\\\\t|       |                                  | `require_once` throw `Error`; a   |\\\\n  2098\\\\t|       |                                  | refused `_once` retries delivery  |\\\\n  2099\\\\t+-------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\\\\n  2100\\\\t| P7-a  | signals: `phpsp` hooks at        | a signal at a `:loop` hook runs   |\\\\n  2101\\\\t|       | `:loop` and `:call`,             | the handler; an empty signal      |\\\\n  2102\\\\t|       | `pcntl_signal`, the safepoint    | stream never parks; budget        |\\\\n  2103\\\\t|       | budget                           | exhaustion raises the pinned      |\\\\n  2104\\\\t|       |                                  | fatal text                        |\\\\n  2105\\\\t+-------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\\\\n  2106\\\\t| P7-b  | ticks: `declare(ticks)`,         | tick counts equal PHP's for the   |\\\\n  2107\\\\t|       | `register_tick_function`; behind | statement fixture                 |\\\\n  2108\\\\t|       | safepoint slice 3                |                                   |\\\\n  2109\\\\t+-------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\\\\n  2110\\\\t| P8    | legacy retirement: the legacy    | the REPL prints `1` for `<?php $x |\\\\n  2111\\\\t|       | test programs in the new corpus, | = 1; echo $x;`; every legacy test |\\\\n  2112\\\\t|       | the demo on checked-in CST       | program passes with corrected     |\\\\n  2113\\\\t|       | packets,                         | expectations; the demo runs its   |\\\\n  2114\\\\t|       | `src/dev/subsystems.edn`         | samples parserless in the         |\\\\n  2115\\\\t|       | retargeted, `yang/php.cljc`      | browser; no reference to the      |\\\\n  2116\\\\t|       | deleted, the compiler revision   | legacy `yang.php` namespace       |\\\\n  2117\\\\t|       | removed from the standard        | remains                           |\\\\n  2118\\\\t|       | catalog                          |                                   |\\\\n  2119\\\\t+-------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\\\\n  2120\\\\t| P9    | builtin surface slices (strings  | each a corpus group with PHP      |\\\\n  2121\\\\t|       | II, arrays II, JSON, math II),   | parity; `eval` shares the         |\\\\n  2122\\\\t|       | `$$`, `extract`, `compact` and   | caller's symbol table; closes     |\\\\n  2123\\\\t|       | the dynamic scope, `eval` as a   | corners C5, C6 (prefer-ref), C11, |\\\\n  2124\\\\t|       | compilation request              | C12, C13                          |\\\\n  2125\\\\t+-------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\\\\n  2126\\\\t\\\\n  2127\\\\tDeferred: `preg_*` (a portable regex engine), `mb_*`, `serialize`, Reflection,\\\\n  2128\\\\tSPL data structures, `DateTime` beyond the time effect, sessions, PDO and\\\\n  2129\\\\tevery `ext/*` surface (bound per ADR 0004), return by reference, `goto`, cycle\\\\n  2130\\\\tcollection (corner C1).\\\\n  2131\\\\t\\\\n  2132\\\\t## 16. The yang.core lift enumeration\\\\n  2133\\\\t\\\\n  2134\\\\tBeyond L1 to L4 (`yang.typescript.md` section 17), PHP requires nothing new.\\\\n  2135\\\\tIt exercises `yang.core.stage` (the ok-check on `:yang.cst/ok`),\\\\n  2425\\\\t|                     | root; optional absent imports are  |                  |\\\\n  2426\\\\t|                     | not publishable.                    |                  |\\\\n  2427\\\\t+---------------------+-------------------------------------+------------------+\\\\n  2428\\\\t| C39 default         | Engine: `spl_autoload_register(null)`| restricted;     |\\\\n  2429\\\\t| autoloader          | installs PHP's default loader. Here:| departure        |\\\\n  2430\\\\t|                     | the call is refused as              | `G-default-      |\\\\n  2431\\\\t|                     | `G-default-autoload`; Composer      | autoload`; class |\\\\n  2432\\\\t|                     | class-map data is allowed, its      | map data remains |\\\\n  2433\\\\t|                     | filesystem lookup is not.           | supported        |\\\\n  2434\\\\t+---------------------+-------------------------------------+------------------+\\\\n  2435\\\\t\\\\n  2436\\\\t### 17.3 Convergence and the blocking rule\\\\n  2437\\\\t\\\\n  2438\\\\t**Claimed rows are normative, and a known-incorrect claimed row blocks.** A\\\\n  2439\\\\trow of the transition table is a statement of Zend's behaviour, fixed from the\\\\n  2440\\\\thandler source and **confirmed by the oracle before the slice that claims it\\\\n  2441\\\\tlands**. After landing, a disagreement between a row and the pinned PHP CLI is\\\\n  2442\\\\ta defect of the row, never of the fixture. A finding that a claimed row\\\\n  2443\\\\tcontradicts Zend blocks landing of any slice whose support profile includes\\\\n  2444\\\\tthat row until either the row is corrected (and its fixture regenerated from\\\\n  2445\\\\tthe oracle), or the Architect explicitly moves the behaviour to section 17.2\\\\n  2446\\\\tas a restricted corner with a departure fixture whose expected value is the\\\\n  2447\\\\trecorded deviation, with the owner's acceptance when the corner touches a\\\\n  2448\\\\tPhase 3 exit criterion. Regenerating a fixture to match an incorrect row is\\\\n  2449\\\\tforbidden: oracle fixtures pin behaviour; they do not legitimize a\\\\n  2450\\\\tcontradictory normative rule.\\\\n  2451\\\\t\\\\n  2452\\\\tThe landing gate is therefore: every claimed-row fixture equals the oracle;\\\\n  2453\\\\tevery corner's departure fixture equals its recorded deviation; no fixture\\\\n  2454\\\\tfailure is unclassified. A new finding lands in one of four bins, in blocking\\\\n  2455\\\\torder:\\\\n  2456\\\\t\\\\n  2457\\\\t1. a defect in a claimed row (blocks);\\\\n  2458\\\\t2. a missing row required by an exit criterion or the C4 profile (blocks);\\\\n  2459\\\\t3. a reclassification request, row to corner (an Architect decision recorded\\\\n  2460\\\\t   in the ledger; blocks until decided);\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_11\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"rg -n '8\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\.6|eval|JVM|parse|compiler' docs/design/yang.antlr.md; rg -n 'php|source|JVM' src/cljc/yang/stage.cljc; rg --files src | rg 'yang/.+(parse|stage)|php'\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":null,\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_11\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"rg -n '8\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\.6|eval|JVM|parse|compiler' docs/design/yang.antlr.md;